WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.000
How does scheduling change with

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.540
multiple queues or cores? Several

3
00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:06.260
queues can represent different service

4
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.460
needs, while several cores supply

5
00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.320
simultaneous execution capacity. Do not

6
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.040
confuse a logical queue partition

7
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.820
with a physical extra processor.

8
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.120
A multilevel queue can use

9
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.180
round robin for interactive foreground

10
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.620
work and first come, first

11
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.880
served for background work. The

12
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.700
operating system still needs an

13
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.000
interqueue policy. Fixed foreground priority

14
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.580
can starve the background queue

15
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.040
even if the policy inside

16
00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.440
each queue is reasonable.

17
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.500
A time-allocation policy might reserve

18
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.320
eighty milliseconds for the foreground

19
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.880
and twenty for the background

20
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.480
in each hundred-millisecond cycle. That

21
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.180
gives eighty percent and twenty

22
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.860
percent of the ideal cycle

23
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.860
budget. It is an illustrative

24
00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:50.560
allocation rule, not a guarantee

25
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.980
about the completion time of

26
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:52.660
every process.

27
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:56.080
With partitioned multicore scheduling, each

28
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.640
process is assigned to a

29
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.480
core and that core schedules

30
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.620
its own work. This permits

31
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.660
different local policies, but a

32
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.340
poor assignment can leave one

33
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.360
core idle while another has

34
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.740
ready processes. Placement and load

35
00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.540
balance become separate problems.

36
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.760
Global scheduling selects from system-wide

37
00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.500
eligible work when a core

38
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.940
becomes available. A process can

39
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.220
run on one core now

40
00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.500
and another later, but one

41
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:25.100
sequential process must not execute

42
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.380
on both at once. Migration

43
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.720
can introduce synchronization and cache

44
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:30.220
costs.

45
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.340
Use the lecture shortest-remaining-time example

46
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.600
with two cores: arrivals zero,

47
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.600
two, four and five, and

48
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.640
bursts seven, four, one and

49
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.560
four. P one begins first,

50
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.140
then P two can run

51
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.640
concurrently when it arrives at

52
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.620
two. At four, the new

53
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.940
one-unit process changes which two

54
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.240
remaining jobs are shortest.

55
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.480
The global choice serves P

56
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.740
three briefly while P one

57
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.520
is paused, then resumes P

58
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.240
one when the short process

59
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.940
completes. P four waits until

60
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.320
a core becomes available at

61
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.940
six and finishes at ten.

62
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.960
P one completes at eight,

63
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:07.960
P two at six, and

64
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.420
P three at five.

65
00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:12.000
The waits are one, zero,

66
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.160
zero and one by identifier,

67
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:15.920
so mean waiting is two

68
00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.420
divided by four, or one

69
00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.820
half. This uses total processor

70
00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:21.840
work per process, not the

71
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.680
time occupied on one particular

72
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.080
core. A migrated process still

73
00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.000
has a single execution requirement.

74
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.660
A trace showing migration proves

75
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.180
that execution moved between cores;

76
00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:34.980
it does not alone prove

77
00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.900
there is exactly one global

78
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.020
ready queue. The slides pair

79
00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.200
partitioned with asymmetric and global

80
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.120
with symmetric scheduling as course

81
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.560
shorthand. Those labels are not

82
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.680
universal synonyms for every implementation.

83
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:49.640
Things to take note of.

84
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.760
Explain policy within queues, selection

85
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.940
between queues, and assignment across

86
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.920
cores separately. Keep arrival eligibility,

87
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.480
remaining execution and one-process-at-a-time constraints

88
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.680
visible when reconstructing a multicore

89
00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:04.340
schedule.
